# Expectation record for 01_integer_conversions/004_narrowing_conversions.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 004_narrowing_conversions
area               = 01_integer_conversions
description        = Narrowing integer conversions at the destination range edge, folded and volatile-runtime
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_audit_flags     = -Wall -Wextra -pedantic -Wshadow -Werror
ub_notes           <<END
Conversion to an unsigned type is defined for every value: the result is reduced modulo
two to the power of the destination width.  The signed narrowing here is performed through
an unsigned intermediate of the destination width, so no implementation-defined signed
conversion of an out-of-range value is relied upon.  No signed overflow, no shift out of
range, no aliasing violation and no uninitialized read occurs.
No header is included and printf is declared by hand, so nothing here depends on a
standard-library header bcc does not ship.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
The warning gate drops -Wconversion and -Wsign-conversion for this program alone.  A
narrowing conversion is the behaviour under test rather than a mistake, and those two
diagnostics exist precisely to reject an accidental narrowing, so with them in force the
program could not be compiled at all and the feature would go untested.  Every other
member of the gate is retained, including -Werror, so no other class of defect in this
program is downgraded to advice.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
narrow_u8=200 narrow_i8=-56 narrow_u16=65336
runtime_u8=200 runtime_i8=-56 runtime_u16=65336
END
